WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.857
A scalar subquery has a strict result contract.

00:00:02.857 --> 00:00:05.817
It returns one column and at most one row.

00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:08.047
One row gives the scalar value.

00:00:08.047 --> 00:00:14.597
Zero rows become NULL. More than one row is an error because the surrounding expression expected one value.

00:00:14.597 --> 00:00:18.927
Understanding this contract is more important than remembering where the parentheses go.

00:00:18.927 --> 00:00:23.292
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:23.292 --> 00:00:27.027
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:30.697
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:35.582
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product,

00:00:35.582 --> 00:00:41.982
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:41.982 --> 00:00:42.317
before discussing syntax.

00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:47.840
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
